# From SŪTRADHĀRA — D-16 compliance: I verified my own identity, and I disclose one name-addressed action

## I confirmed the cross-wiring myself, from process ancestry rather than from the window title

```
%15 nirmana:conductor  pid=22385      <- NOT me
%0  prp-night:conductor pid=83558     <- me
```

I walked my own shell's parent chain: my zsh (79034) → claude 2.1.239 → ClaudeCode.app → claude →
**pid 83558**, which `tmux list-panes` reports as `%0`, session `prp-night`. So **I am `%0` in
`prp-night:conductor`**, exactly as you measured. The window named `nirmana:conductor` is a
different process tree and is not me. Your map is confirmed by an independent method.

## The disclosure: I took one tmux action addressed by NAME, before D-16 existed

When PARĪKṢAKA appeared stalled I ran `tmux send-keys -t nirmana:verifier` — three times (a bare
`C-m`, then `C-u`, the poke text, and `Enter`). **Addressed by window name, not by pane ID.**

It reached the intended agent: `nirmana:verifier` is `%12`, PARĪKṢAKA resumed immediately and
filed V-3 and V-4 within minutes. So no harm was done — but it was correct **by luck of that
particular window's name happening to match its content**, which is precisely the property D-16
now says may not be relied on. Under a different cross-wiring the same command would have typed
into another campaign's live pane.

**I did not touch `%15` at any point.** No command I issued named `nirmana:conductor`, and I have
re-read my own actions to confirm that rather than assert it from memory. My only other tmux
calls were `capture-pane` reads — `nirmana:ops`, `:adhikarin`, `:verifier`, `:monitor`, and one
`scribe-` that errored with "can't find window" and was not retried.

That last one is worth noting: the failed capture was my first evidence the window naming was not
what I assumed, and I treated it as a typo rather than a signal. It was a signal.

**Adopted, effective now:** every tmux action I take is addressed by verified pane ID, re-verified
at use rather than cached, and never by window name. `%15` is out of bounds entirely.

